  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce final detailed technical tutorial. Need ensure complete sentences, avoid repetition. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows. Concrete examples and tradeoffs, common performance pitfalls.\n\nNeed think structure. Could be tutorial with sections. Need maybe use PostgreSQL/MySQL examples. Need explain SQL execution pipeline: client sends query, parser, analyzer, rewriter, planner/optimizer, executor, storage engine, transaction manager, result set. Include indexes: B-tree, hash, GIN/GiST, covering, partial, composite, index-only scans, index condition pushdown, statistics, cardinality estimates. Joins: nested loop, hash, merge, join order, dynamic programming, memoization, lateral, subqueries, decorrelation, common table expressions, materialized vs inlined. Transactions: ACID, MVCC, locks, isolation levels, snapshots, write-ahead log, two-phase commit, deadlocks, concurrency. Returning rows: fetch protocol, cursor, streaming, materialization, row format, network, client. Performance pitfalls: missing indexes, bad selectivity, index bloat, statistics stale, implicit type casts, functions on indexed columns, OR conditions, NOT IN with NULLs, large IN lists, N+1, unbounded result sets, ORDER BY/LIMIT without index, sorting spills, hash join memory, lock contention, long transactions, autocommit, transaction isolation, deadlocks, over-indexing, write amplification, partitioning, sharding, connection overhead, prepared statements, caching.\n\nNeed avoid repeating. Use complete sentences. Could include SQL examples and EXPLAIN. Need maybe mention specific database differences. Need be detailed but not too long? Desired oververbosity 9. We can write long tutorial.\n\nNeed ensure no repetition. Could use headings.\n\nLet's outline:\n\nTitle: How a Relational Database Executes a SQL Query\n\nIntro: A query is not executed line by line; DBMS transforms it into physical plan.\n\n1. End-to-end pipeline\n- Client sends SQL over protocol.\n- Parser: lexical analysis, syntax tree.\n- Analyzer/semantic: resolve tables/columns, types, permissions, rewrite views, CTEs, subqueries.",
